body:
This is a feature for instantsearch.js V2 -- breaking change

Right now, we have a function that can be given to the instantsearch factory called searchFunction. This function is given a helper that can't be modified, only the search function can be really used. Instead we could only provide a single function `search` as a parameter.

## API proposal

```js
instantsearch({
  searchFunction: (search, searchParameters) => {
     search(searchParameters.setQuery(''));
  };
});
```

In this example, the function will prevent the query to be set.

comments.body:
I've found myself confused on when I need to use `instantsearch.helper` and when I need to use the shallow-clone of it in `searchFunction`.

Because the `searchFunction` param is just a shallow clone with `.search()`, if I need any other methods (e.g. `setQueryParameter`), I need to use `instantsearch.helper`. However, once I actually `.search`, I need to use the helper from the `searchFunction` param, or the search won't work.

Here's an example. I want to prevent initial searching, listen for a map event and `setQueryParameter`, and then search. To do this, I have to juggle between both of the helpers, which seems icky and confusing.

```
var myMap = Leaflet.map('my-map');
var clonedHelper;

var instantSearch = new InstantSearch({
  searchFunction: (helper) => {
    clonedHelper = helper;
  }
});

map.on('moveend', (event) => {
  instantSearch.helper.setQueryParameter('insideBoundingBox', '...');
  clonedHelper.search();

  // Doesn't work
  // instantSearch.helper.search();
});
```

Hi @timkelty Actually we failed you here, we are not providing enough resources for the kind of use case you have. The correct way (as instantsearch.js is designed) is to create a custom widget for you map component:

```js
var mapWidget = {
  init: function({helper}) {
    this.map = Leaflet.map('my-map');
    map.on('moveend', (event) => {
      helper.setQueryParameter('insideBoundingBox', '...').search();
    });
  }
}
```

I hope this will help you.

After using this method a few times, using the fake helper while wanting to change the values before doing a search feels terribly clunky. However JS has prototypal inheritance and we could leverage it to provide a lightweight helper that actually triggers the search.

New proposal would be:

```javascript
instantsearch({
  searchFunction: (helper) => {
     helper.setQuery('new value').search());
  };
});
```

This means that it will feel like the original helper with the same interface and we won't need to actually get the helper instance on the instantsearch object.
